1. Download the official Open MPI 4.1.8 source:

```
mkdir -p "$HOME/local/src"
cd "$HOME/local/src"

curl -fLO https://download.open-mpi.org/release/open-mpi/v4.1/openmpi-4.1.8.tar.gz

tar -xzf openmpi-4.1.8.tar.gz
cd openmpi-4.1.8
```

2. Configure, compile, and install:

```
./configure --prefix="$HOME/local/openmpi-4.1.8" \
    CC=/usr/bin/gcc FC=/usr/bin/gfortran \
    --enable-mpi-fortran --disable-mpi-cxx
```

After configuration succeeds:

```
/bin/make -j2
```

After compilation succeeds:

```
/bin/make install
```

This uses the standard source-installation procedure with a user-owned installation prefix. [Open MPI installation instructions (https://docs.open-mpi.org/en/main/installing-open-mpi/quickstart.html)](<https://docs.open-mpi.org/en/main/installing-open-mpi/quickstart.html>)

3. Activate your installation:

```
export PATH="$HOME/local/openmpi-4.1.8/bin:$PATH"
export LD_LIBRARY_PATH="$HOME/local/openmpi-4.1.8/lib${LD_LIBRARY_PATH:+:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH}"
hash -r

command -v mpifort
mpifort --showme:compile
```

The paths should now point inside **your home directory**.

4. Test that `USE mpi` compiles:

```
printf 'program test_mpi\nuse mpi\nend program test_mpi\n' > /tmp/ommair_test_mpi.f90

mpifort -c /tmp/ommair_test_mpi.f90 -o /tmp/ommair_test_mpi.o
```

5. In your simulation source directory, change the Makefile’s compiler from:

```
FC = /bin/mpif90.openmpi
```

to:

```
FC = mpifort
```

If the compiler variable has another name, change that variable instead. Then rebuild using your Makefile’s clean target, if available:

```
/bin/make clean
/bin/make
```

**Use this same local Open MPI installation when running the executable.** Include the two `export` lines above in your job script so compilation and execution use matching MPI libraries.
